            "source_quote": "καὶ τοσοῦτον προαίρεσιν πόθον ἔχοντα διισχυρίζεσθαι, ὡς καὶ ἐνίας τῶν γραφῶν περικοπὰς σαφῶς καταλεγούσας αὐτοῦ τολμᾶν ψευδεῖς λέγειν",
            "english_quote": "and to insist that he has so strong a desire for free choice that you even dare to call false certain passages of the writings that plainly accuse him.",
            "issue": "The draft makes the Creator’s desire for free choice the content of Peter’s insistence. Merely attaching ἔχοντα to τὸν δημιουργόν does not establish that construction: the rendering also requires διισχυρίζεσθαι to govern an indirect assertion expressed by ἔχοντα, rather than functioning as the infinitive specifying the longing to insist. The contextual construction with ἔχοντα resuming σε accounts directly for both Peter’s longing to insist and the following degree/result clause about Peter’s daring. The notes disclose the referent alternative, but that disclosure does not support the additional grammatical restructuring in the main text. This is a rendering problem, not evidence of corrupt print.",
            "suggested_revision": "Use a construction referring the longing and insistence to Peter, for example: “and that, in your resolve, you have so great a longing to insist on this that you even dare to call false certain passages of the writings that plainly accuse him.” Explain the accusative-of-respect interpretation of προαίρεσιν and why the connection between Peter’s longing, insistence, and daring favors this attachment. Preserve the material attachment alternative without presenting the present Creator-desire rendering as established. The replacement requires fresh review.",
            "severity": "major"
          }
